section{

hgroup{
    h2#section_i {
                                § I. Mario Markus's λ-Diagrams
    }
    a[href=#contents]{ ↩ contents }
}

$header { Mario Markus's λ-Diagrams
$description
An account of the λ-Diagram and its history.
}

The a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyapunov_fractal]{Lyapunov fractal} was
introduced by a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mario_Markus]{Mario Markus} under the supervision of
a[href=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/anie.200390466]{Benno Hess} in the 1989
paper cite{a[href=https://doi.org/10.1016/0097-8493(89)90019-8]{Lyapunov exponents of the logistic
map with periodic forcing}}. It is a visualization of the iterated a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logistic_map]{logistic map} $m { f_n{(x)} = r_n dot x dot {(1 - x)}} with a periodically varying parameter $m {r_n} taking on two values $m { a, b} according to a rule specified as a string of i{A} $nospace s and i{B} $nospace s. For instance the string i{ABA} corresponds to the sequence $m { r_0 = a, r_1 = b, r_2 = a, r_3 = a, r_4 = b, r_5 = a,...} which repeats ad infinitum. The values of $m{ a} and $m{ b} vary with the pixel coordinates within the diagrams. Starting typically from $m{ x_0 = 0.5}, $noted { the a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyapunov_exponent]{Lyapunov exponent} $m {  lambda } is computed for each such parametrization: $note In earlier drafts of this article, I included a section building up the formula for the Lyapunov exponent from more basic ideas, for those unfamiliar with it. That section grew quite long, will take much more work to complete, and is distinct in purpose from the rest of this article. For these reasons I have opted to make it a separate piece, which I intend to publish at a later date. }

$m{block lambda = underover lim {n arrowr inf} {} end 1/n underover sum {i=1} {n} end log{|f'_i{(x_i)}|} }



These exponents are then mapped to a color gradient. A negative exponent indicates convergence of perturbed trajectories, while a positive exponent indicates divergence of perturbed trajectories. $noted { Speaking less precisely $note See a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyapunov_exponent#Perron_effects_of_largest_Lyapunov_exponent_sign_inversion]{ the discussion of Perron's counterexample on the Lyapunov exponent's Wikipedia page} for details on why em{stability vs chaos} is an imprecise characterization. }, this can be interpreted as em{stability vs. chaos}. Often, distinct color schemes are applied to the positive and negative cases. $noted { The values are unbounded $note Unbounded below, at least. Inside the parameter range $m { [0,4] cross [0,4] } the Lyapunov exponent takes on a maximum value which, in the case of the classic i{AB} fractal, is $m { lambda = log 2 }, at $m { a = b = 4}. }, so some normalization is required.


The Lyapunov exponent is where the Lyapunov fractal gets its traditional name. Since a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aleksandr_Lyapunov]{Aleksandr Lyapunov} never lived to see these fractals, they are sometimes called $scare{Markus-Lyapunov fractals} instead. $noted { Markus himself, however, in his 2007 book $noted { cite{a[href=https://archive.org/details/chartsforpredict0000mark/]{Charts for Prediction and Chance}}, $note Subtitled i{Dazzling Diagrams on Your PC}, a truly marvelous volume replete with technical insights, a large number of gorgeous renders, and Markus's own rendering software on a CD-ROM. You can view a number of the images from it on a[href=https://www.mariomarkus.com/hp4.html]{Markus's website}. I highly recommend taking a look; the images are at the bottom of the linked page. } uses the term $scare{λ-diagrams}, as λ is the traditional symbol used for Lyapunov exponents and strictly speaking not em{every} such diagram is a fractal. $note Chapter 10, cite{Are the λ-Diagrams Fractals?} Page 213, in my copy. } While the term $scare{Lyapunov fractal} enjoys more widespread use on the web, I choose to follow the convention of i{Charts}.

At time of writing, most of the information one can find on the internet about the $scare{Lyapunov fractal} focuses exclusively on the variants based on the logistic map. This is a tragedy, as the concept of Lyapunov exponent is much more general, and so the full aesthetic range of these diagrams often goes overlooked.  As early as his 1990 publication cite{a[href=https://doi.org/10.1063/1.4822940]{Chaos in Maps with Continuous and Discontinuous Maxima}}, Markus had begun exploring $noted{a different, discontinuous modification $note
The discontinuous logistic map is defined piecewise as follows:
$m {
    ident alpha:~ x r n
    text if:~
    op > leq:≤ + ( ) - = ·
    in
    x_{n+1} = r_n · x_n · {(1 - x_n)} if x_n > 0.5
    x_{n+1} = r_n · x_n · {(1 - x_n)} + 0.25 {(alpha - 1)} {(r_n - 2)} if x_n leq 0.5
}
Wherein α is a real parameter, typically just a little less than 1. }
of the logistic map; in i{Charts} he really takes the concept and runs with it, applying the visualization technique across more than 39 different dynamical systems. And of course, Markus is not alone in this: anyone who spends much of their time engaging with dynamical systems literature will come across a good number of Lyapunov diagrams.

The λ-diagrams sit in the overlap between art and science. While they can be appreciated with or without care for their $scare{meaning}, to engage with the underlying math is to experience their beauty more fully; and while these and related techniques can help us understand the dynamics of real physical phenomena, it would be sad to appreciate them em{solely} for their utility.

details { summary { Pseudocode, Logistic AB λ-Diagram }
pre { code#pseudocode {
sequence = "AB"
x_0 = 0.5
max_iterations = 4000
skip_iterations = 600

def get_r(iteration):
    index = iteration % length(sequence)
    return r_A if sequence[index] == "A" else r_B

def rolling_average(current_average, next_value, iteration):
    if is_nan_or_inf(current_average):
        return current_average
    next_average = current_average + (next_value - current_average) / iteration
    return next_average

def colorize(lyapunov_exponent):
    # lots of ways you can implement this
    pass

def compute_pixel_color(x_coordinate, y_coordinate):
    r_A = x_coordinate
    r_B = y_coordinate

    x = x_0
    average_log_derivative = 0

    for iteration in range(max_iterations):
        r = get_r(iteration)
        x_next = r * x * (1 - x)

        if iteration >= skip_iterations:
            derivative = r - 2 * r * x
            log_derivative = log(abs(derivative))
            average_log_derivative = rolling_average(
                average_log_derivative,
                log_derivative,
                iteration - skip_iterations + 1
            )

        x = x_next

    return colorize(average_log_derivative)
} } }

$footnotes { Section I Footnotes }


}













section{

hgroup{
    h2#section_ii {
                                § II. Sequence Offsets
    }
    a[href=#contents]{ ↩ contents }
}

Consider a λ-diagram with periodic forcing sequence i{AB}, and another with sequence i{BA}. If all we could see was a segment in the middle of the ongoing sequence: i{...ABABABABAB...}. We wouldn't be able to tell which of these systems we were looking at. And indeed when we look at the i{AB} and i{BA} logistic λ-diagrams, we find they are substantially similar:

$figure[lyapunov|ab_ba_comparison]{ Comparison, Logistic λ-diagram AB (left) vs BA (right) }

They disagree only in a small region, and basically only on which $scare{overlapping stalk} is $scare{on top}. More generally than the i{AB}/ i{BA} case, the same sort of pattern holds for any sequences which only differ up to em{starting offset within the sequence}. For instance, the sequences i{ABA}, i{BAA}, i{AAB} all belong to the same family, the middle of the sequence looking something like i{...ABAABAABAABA...}.

In the interactive renderer, you can quickly cycle through these offsets by setting the i{offset mode} to $scare{single} and then adjusting the $scare{seq offset} slider.

a[href=https://doi.org/10.1063/1.4822940]{Chaos in Maps with Continuous and Discontinuous Maxima}, in addition to investigating



The other idea he introduced was to apply a little (pseudo-)random variation to the sequence of A's and B's. The exact details of how that variation is implemented are a little bit hard to parse, in my opinion, but I believe I have settled on an implementation which closely matches the results of Markus's paper. TODO describe this explain the parameter

A somewhat more common modification is to include a third value, C, corresponding either to a third spatial dimension or time. a[href=https://gidden.net/2017/03/09/exploring-lyapunov-space/]{Tom Gidden's 2017 blog post} provides a more in-depth description of that, and a[href=https://www.youtube.com/shorts/KAK6IJMbhwI]{this short animation by @desden0va on youtube} makes the idea intuitive.

}




section {

hgroup{
    h2#section_iii {
                                § III. Observations of the Logistic λ-Diagram
    }
    a[href=#contents]{ ↩ contents }
}

The original paper concerns itself only with A and B values in the range [0,4]. However, there is no technical constraint that prevents us from peeking outside that box. When we do, it becomes visually apparent that this range is not entirely arbitrary (a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logistic_map#Characterization_of_the_logistic_map]{the logistic map remains finite there}). The crisp edge of this "blessed" square stands out prominently. Nevertheless, there is some interesting structure outside of it. Some of the superstable curves poke out into the void of divergence. A smaller, distorted square exists where A and B are both negative.

h3[style=text-decoration:underline]{Algebraic Curves}

Qualitatively, the fractal appears to be structured along a "skeleton" of precise curves, the so-called "superstable" curves, where the Lyapunov exponent diverges to $m {  -inf }. We can actually produce exact algebraic descriptions of these curves!

Consider the formula for the Lyapunov exponent: $m {  underover lim {n arrowr inf} {} end 1/n underover sum {i=1} {n} end log{|d/{d x} f_i{(x_i)}|} }

Since $m {  log{|x|} } diverges to $m {  -inf } as x approaches 0, we have that the entire sum must diverge when $m {  d/{d x} f_n{(x_n)} = 0 }. Given $m {  x_0 = 1/2}, we can apply the logistic formula with alternating parameters $m { a} and $m { b} repeatedly and obtain $m {  d/{d x} f_n{(x_n)} } as a multivariate polynomial in $m { a} and $m { b}.

Because $m {  x_0 = 1/2 }, the first iteration of our iterative process actually has derivative 0 uniformly across all r-values, which is why the sum in our Lyapunov exponent calculation begins at index 1 instead of 0. Anyway, assuming the basic "AB" sequence, and choosing scaling factors $m {  c_n } so as to produce polynomials with the same zeros but with integer coefficients, the first few curves $m {  P_n = c_n dot d/{d x} f_n{(x_n)} = 0 } are as follows:

$m { block
P_1 = {- a} + 2 = 0

P_2 = a^2 b - 4 ab + 8 = 0

P_3 = a^5b^2 - 8 a^4b^2 + 16 a^3 b^2 + 16 a^3 b - 64 a^2 b + 128 = 0

P_4 = a^10b^5 - 16a^9b^5 + 96a^8b^5 + 32a^8b^4 - 256a^7b^5 - 384a^7b^4 + 256a^6b^5 + 1536a^6b^4 + 256a^6b^3 - 2048a^5b^4 - 1792a^5b^3 + 2048a^4b^3 + 4096a^3b^3 + 4096a^3b^2 - 16384a^2b^2 + 32768 = 0
}

While the first few of these can be worked out by hand (an exercise for the reader, of course), they grow in complexity very quickly. By the sixth iteration, there are already hundreds of terms, and by the tenth, a plaintext file containing the formula takes up several megabytes. I had an LLM agent use SymPy to produce the first ten iterations of polynomials, and while I didn't look at the code at all, the task was straightforward and the plots look correct:

