$comment {
$figure[
    lyapunov/curve_composite: Composite of first 10 iterations.
    lyapunov/curve_d01: P_1 = 0
    lyapunov/curve_d02: P_2 = 0
    lyapunov/curve_d03: P_3 = 0
] {

}}

You can see how these curves accumulate and form the "backbone" of the fractal in the interactive renderer by setting "skip" to 1 and "iterations" to 2, and then gradually incrementing the iteration count. The negative-infinite exponents pleasingly trace out exactly these algebraic curves. It's also interesting to note the alternation between two different qualitative types of curve on even and odd iterations. I have not put much thought into that, but I'm sure there's an interesting reason for it.

Of course, in the original papers, the first 600 iterations are skipped. Some of the algebraic curves from early on are lost due to this, but others reassert themselves no matter how late the accumulation begins. Indeed even with "skip" set to 1 in the renderer you can see that many of the lines appear to fade away quite quickly as the iteration count grows. In reality the limit does still diverge at every point on any of these curves, it's just that the ones that fade have vanishing influence on nearby points. The curves that very em{visibly} remain are those that are included in infinitely many later curves. If it is only these "limiting superstable curves" one cares about, however, there is no finite number of iterations we can skip to exclude all the rest. But I don't feel like pursuing that line of thought any further here and now.

Anyway, I think this is a pretty neat decomposition of much of the structure of the fractal.

$figure[lyapunov|decomp_stalks] { Decomposition of the Logistic λ-diagram AB (Offset-Minimum mode) into $scare{stalks}. }

$figure[lyapunov|decomp_crosses] { Decomposition of the Logistic λ-diagram AB (Offset-Minimum mode) into $scare{crosses}. }

}


section {

h2#controls_notes { ❖ Renderer Controls }

h3{Mouse Controls}

You can click and drag to translate the view around.

Scroll up to zooms in, and down to zoom out.

There is a grid icon in the upper-left corner of the canvas. Clicking there will toggle the overlay.

h3{Context Menu}

If you right-click anywhere on the canvas, it'll open a context menu that lets you save the canvas, or an upscaled version which can be 2, 3, or 4 times the resolution on each axis (16x total image size). Note that depending on your settings, some of these upscaled renders can take a few moments, or even end up getting killed by the browser for taking too long. If you have a very large canvas already, some of the upscaled options may fail to run, due to technical limitations on buffer sizes. I have some workarounds in mind for these issues, but that is not a development priority at present.

Another option in the menu is to toggle the visibility of the control panel, to get a little bit more canvas space.

Finally, you can exit the renderer entirely. This will leave behind a blank canvas, where you can load up arbitrary modules. It's a bit of a work-in-progress system and I won't try to document it in full here. Think of it as falling into the backrooms. The main thing you'll probably want to do is get back out, by opening the context menu and selecting $scare{Lyapunov}. Note that you can also create resizable regions with the $scare{row} and $scare{col} options and then create multiple renderers, or just have one that you can conveniently resize to the aspect ratio of your liking.

h3{Parameters}

sequence: The strings of As and Bs that defines the periodic forcing sequence

x_0: The starting value for the iterative process, at all points

iterations: N in the formula

skip: M in the formula

offset mode: How should offsets of the sequence be handled?
    Single: Just one offset is considered; a slider is provided to sweep across them
    Minimum, Maximum, Average: The Lyapunov exponent is computed for all offsets, and the minimum, maximum, or average is taken, respectively
    min_abs: Minimum of the absolute value of lambda
    count_distinct: Counts the number of distinct values taken by lambda across the offsets, where distinctness is up to a threshold, epsilon. This mode is kinda janky, a lot of what can be seen with it is due to numerical issues. I left it in because it can produce interesting visuals.
    avgAbsDiff: Compute lambdas for all offsets, compute absolute differences between all pairs of them, take the average of all those differences.



h3{Keyboard Shortcuts}

You can press F to toggle fullscreen mode.

h3{Presets}

Presets in the interactive renderer have been set up to approximately reproduce the figures from Markus's 1989 and 1990 papers. The renders are not 100% faithful, but they are best-effort. The papers specify the r-value sequences and the coordinate bounds of each render. They do not specify the starting offset in the sequence, which, as I discovered in the course of implementing the presets, is not always 0. This page's interactive renderer has a variable width and height, so I calculated a center point and zoom value that would approximately match the original bounds. It may be necessary to resize one's window, zoom, and pan around, in order to get a more exact match to what is present in the original papers. Some of the original renders also exhibit anisotropic stretching. My renderer does not at present have the capacity to replicate this effect. The original papers specify the $m { x_0} values for many of the figures, but I found that some visual features (for example, in 1989 Fig. 1(a)) could not be reproduced without modifying $m { x_0} slightly. I opted to make this modification, prioritizing visual similarity with the original visualization over precise copying of parameters.


}


